# Exercise: Accepting Connections
#
# `net.tcp.accept` blocks the calling strand until a client connects,
# then returns a fresh socket for that connection.
#
# Stack effect: ( Socket -- Socket Bool )
#   ( server-socket -- client-socket success )
#
# Note: accept *replaces* the server socket on the stack with the
# client socket. To keep the server socket for later (closing it,
# or accepting another connection), `dup` it first:
#
#     server-sock dup net.tcp.accept   # ( server client success )
#
# But accept only returns when *somebody* connects. In production
# that somebody is another machine; in a test we spawn a strand
# inside this program to do the connecting. This is the real
# loopback pattern, the same one that runs the patch-seq net.tcp
# integration tests:
#
#   1. Main strand listens on a port.
#   2. Main strand spawns a `connector` strand.
#   3. Main strand `dup net.tcp.accept` — parks until the connector
#      runs and calls `net.tcp.connect`.
#   4. Strands cooperate: while main is parked in accept, the
#      runtime gives the connector a chance to make its connect call.
#   5. Both sides close their sockets.
#
# The order matters: main calls listen *before* spawn, so the
# spawned connector can't fail to find the listener.
#
# Your task: fill in the spawn + accept dance in the success branch.
# When done, delete the marker line below.

# I AM NOT DONE

: connector ( -- )
    "127.0.0.1" 18262 net.tcp.connect
    [ net.tcp.close drop ]
    [ drop ]
    if
;

: test-accept ( -- )
    18262 net.tcp.listen
    [ # ( server ) — write the spawn+accept here:
      #   1. spawn the `connector` word as a strand (drop its id)
      #   2. dup the server socket and call net.tcp.accept
      #   3. close BOTH sockets (server and client) and leave `true`
      #      on the stack for the assertion. Use a nested if on the
      #      accept-success Bool — close the server even when accept
      #      fails so the listener doesn't leak.
      drop false                # placeholder — replace this block
    ]
    [ drop false ]
    if

    # Do not edit below this line
    test.assert
;
